\section{Setting up the pre/post-test, literately}
\label{app:quiz}

This appendix is a literate program \autocite{LiterateProgramming}: the prose
and the code are one and the same source, woven into the text you are reading
and tangled into the files the computer runs.
It implements the pre/post-test of \cref{sec:method-instruments}: a pair of
quizzes that measure whether students discern the candidate critical aspects
of \cref{sec:model,sec:staging,sec:branches,sec:remotes} before and after the
course module, and it prepares their results for analysis.
Three files are tangled from this section: [[quiz-knowledge-start.json]] and
[[quiz-knowledge-end.json]], the descriptions of the two quizzes that the
[[canvaslms]] tool pushes to Canvas, and [[analyze_quiz.py]], which fetches
the results back and summarises them.

\subsection{The two quizzes for Canvas}

The learning platform of the course is Canvas, and the [[canvaslms]]
command-line tool creates the quizzes from their JSON descriptions:
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms quizzes create -c datintro26 -f quiz-knowledge-start.json
canvaslms quizzes create -c datintro26 -f quiz-knowledge-end.json
\end{minted}
There are two quizzes because the same knowledge questions are asked twice:
once at the start of the module, to measure what students discern before it
(\cref{sec:method-instruments}), and once at the end, to measure the gain.
The knowledge items are therefore defined once and appear in both quizzes;
the two differ only in what opens them and in their instructions.
The titles name the two administrations so that students and analysis alike
can tell them apart.

The start quiz opens with a consent item---participation in the study is
opt-in, so we ask before collecting anything---and then the shared knowledge
items.

<<[[quiz-knowledge-start.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    "title": "Git quiz: what you know already",
    "instructions": "<p>This quiz checks what you already know about Git, so that we can adapt the course. The questions show git commands and their output; if you have not used Git before, answer as well as you can&mdash;guessing is fine, and a low score is expected and completely okay. You will see your score afterwards, but not the correct answers&mdash;the course will teach you those. The score does not affect your grade. Please answer on your own, without help from others or from tools.</p>",
    "points_possible": 10,
    <<scheduling settings>>
    "quiz_settings": {
      <<interaction settings>>
      <<graded result view>>
    }
  },
  "items": [
    <<item: consent>>,
    <<knowledge items>>
  ]
}
@

The end quiz opens instead with a question of its own: whether the student
prepared for the quiz.
We ask because preparation would confound the gain measure---a student who
reviews the material the night before measures preparation, not what the
course left behind.

<<[[quiz-knowledge-end.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    "title": "Git quiz: what you know now",
    "instructions": "<p>This quiz repeats the Git questions from the start-of-course quiz, so that we can see what you have learned during the course. You will see your score afterwards, but not the correct answers. The score does not affect your grade. Please answer on your own, without preparation, help or tools.</p>",
    "points_possible": 10,
    <<scheduling settings>>
    "quiz_settings": {
      <<interaction settings>>
      <<graded result view>>
    }
  },
  "items": [
    <<item: preparation>>,
    <<knowledge items>>
  ]
}
@

Both quizzes are diagnostics: there is no time limit, a single attempt
suffices, and answers are never shuffled---the order of the choices is
meaningful in several items, and the order of the items is fixed so that the
two administrations line up.
The shared settings come in two chunks---scheduling and interaction---and
each chunk is a flat list of keys, so every brace opens and closes in the
chunk that uses it: the [[quiz_settings]] object opens and closes in the quiz
descriptions above.

<<scheduling settings>>=
"time_limit": null,
"due_at": null,
"unlock_at": null,
"lock_at": null,
@

<<interaction settings>>=
"shuffle_answers": false,
"shuffle_questions": false,
"has_time_limit": false,
"one_at_a_time_type": "none",
"allow_backtracking": true,
"multiple_attempts_enabled": false,
"result_view_restricted": true,
"display_items": true,
"display_item_response": true,
@

Both quizzes show the points awarded---how well the student did---but neither
per-item correctness nor the correct answers: the same questions must measure
the gain at the end of the course, so they have to stay fresh.

<<graded result view>>=
  "display_item_response_correctness": false,
  "display_item_correct_answer": false,
  "display_points_awarded": true,
  "display_points_possible": true
@

