\subsection{The knowledge items}

Each knowledge item tests one candidate critical aspect from
\cref{sec:model,sec:staging,sec:branches,sec:remotes}, and each is worth one
point: we measure discernment by counting correct answers.
Every item is a single-answer choice question whose distractors are, wherever
the literature documents one, an actual misconception---so a student who has
not yet discerned the aspect is drawn to the distractor that expresses the
model they are substituting instead.
Following \citeauthor{NCOL}'s discussion of assessment, the stems do not point
out the aspect under test; discerning it is exactly what we measure
\autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}.

The items are numbered from position 11 so that their placement is
independent of the quiz they appear in: Canvas orders by position, gaps are
fine, and the numbering leaves room for the quiz-specific opening item
(consent or preparation) at position 1.

<<knowledge items>>=
<<item: what a commit keeps>>,
<<item: after you save>>,
<<item: where earlier versions live>>,
<<item: what git add does>>,
<<item: two versions at once>>,
<<item: seeing another branch>>,
<<item: deleting a branch>>,
<<item: what merging does>>,
<<item: what git push does>>,
<<item: why they cannot see it>>
@

\subsubsection{The commit model}

The first item tests that a commit is a \emph{snapshot} of the whole tree,
not a stored \emph{difference} (\cref{sec:model}).
The key distractor is the commit-as-diff reading---that a commit records only
the changed lines---together with a partial-snapshot reading that only the
edited file is kept.

<<item: what a commit keeps>>=
