\subsection{Analysing the results}
\label{app:quiz-analysis}

Canvas compiles the responses into a student-analysis report: a CSV file with
one row per student and one column per item, keyed by the item titles above.
The analysis program fetches that report itself---we know the course and the
quiz titles, so nothing needs to be clicked or downloaded by hand---using the
same [[canvaslms]] machinery as the command-line tool.
It then tabulates the two unscored control items and summarises the score
distribution of the graded knowledge items.
All items here are closed choices, so there is nothing to code by hand and no
coding sheet to prepare.

<<[[analyze_quiz.py]]>>=
"""Fetch and summarise the Git pre/post-test results.

The two quizzes are defined in quiz-knowledge-start.json and
quiz-knowledge-end.json, both tangled from the same literate source (the
appendix of the paper).  The results come straight from Canvas via
canvaslms; the item titles are the column keys.

Requires canvaslms (and its configured credentials).
"""

import argparse
import collections
import csv
import os
import statistics
import sys

<<the course and the item titles are the keys>>
<<fetch the results from Canvas>>
<<tabulate the closed items>>
<<analysis main>>

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
@

The course name and the item titles are the join keys between the tangled
files: if a title changes in a quiz, this dictionary is the only other place
to change---and they live in the same source, side by side.
Canvas prefixes the report's columns with item identifiers, so we match
columns by containment rather than equality.

<<the course and the item titles are the keys>>=
COURSE = "datintro26"  # TODO: confirm the actual course instance
QUIZ_TITLES = {
    "start": "Git quiz: what you know already",
    "end": "Git quiz: what you know now",
}
STUDENT = "name"
CLOSED_ITEMS = ["Consent", "Preparation"]


def column_for(title, fieldnames):
    """Return the report column whose name contains the item title."""
    return next((name for name in fieldnames if title in name), None)
@

\subsubsection{Fetching the report from Canvas}

Just as we create the quizzes with [[canvaslms]], we extract the results with
it: the same package provides the credentials, the course lookup and the
report machinery, so the program reuses them instead of reimplementing the
Canvas API calls.
The report is generated on demand by Canvas, so we request it, wait for it,
and download the CSV it points to.

<<fetch the results from Canvas>>=
def fetch_results(which):
    """Download the student-analysis rows for the start or end quiz."""
    import appdirs
    import requests
    import canvaslms.cli
    import canvaslms.cli.login
    from canvasapi import Canvas
    from canvaslms.cli.quizzes import (
        create_new_quiz_report,
        filter_quizzes,
        is_new_quiz,
        poll_progress,
    )

    config_file = os.path.join(
        appdirs.user_config_dir("canvaslms"), "config.json"
    )
    config = canvaslms.cli.read_configuration(config_file)
    hostname, token = canvaslms.cli.login.load_credentials(config)
    if not hostname.startswith("http"):
        hostname = f"https://{hostname}"
    canvas = Canvas(hostname, token)

    course = next(
        c for c in canvas.get_courses()
        if COURSE in (c.name or "") or COURSE in (c.course_code or "")
    )
    quiz = next(filter_quizzes([course], QUIZ_TITLES[which]))
    if not is_new_quiz(quiz):
        sys.exit(f"quiz {quiz.title!r} is not a New Quiz")

    progress = poll_progress(
        create_new_quiz_report(course, quiz.id, canvas._Canvas__requester)
    )
    url = progress.results.get("url") if progress else None
    if not url:
        sys.exit("Canvas did not produce the student-analysis report")
    response = requests.get(url)
    response.raise_for_status()
    return list(csv.DictReader(response.text.splitlines()))
@

\subsubsection{Summaries}

Tabulating a closed item is counting its answer frequencies; we print one
table per item present in the report (the consent item exists only in the
start quiz, the preparation item only in the end quiz).
The knowledge items are graded, so Canvas reports a score column, which we
summarise rather than tabulate---the pre/post gain lives in that
distribution.

<<tabulate the closed items>>=
def tabulate(rows, item):
    """Print the answer frequencies for one closed item."""
    column = column_for(item, rows[0].keys())
    if column is None:
        return
    counts = collections.Counter(row[column] for row in rows)
    print(f"\n{item}")
    for answer, count in counts.most_common():
        print(f"  {answer or '(no answer)'}: {count}")


def summarize_scores(rows):
    """Print the score distribution, if the report has scores."""
    column = column_for("score", rows[0].keys())
    if column is None:
        return
    scores = [float(row[column]) for row in rows if row[column]]
    if scores:
        print(f"\nscores: n={len(scores)}"
              f", mean={statistics.mean(scores):.1f}"
              f", median={statistics.median(scores):.1f}"
              f", max={max(scores):.0f}")
@

The main function ties the pieces together: fetch (or read) the report for
the chosen quiz, tabulate its control item, and summarise the knowledge
scores.

<<analysis main>>=
def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)
    parser.add_argument("--quiz", choices=QUIZ_TITLES, default="start",
                        help="which quiz to analyse")
    parser.add_argument("--results",
                        help="read this CSV instead of fetching from Canvas")
    args = parser.parse_args()

    if args.results:
        with open(args.results, newline="") as infile:
            rows = list(csv.DictReader(infile))
    else:
        rows = fetch_results(args.quiz)
    if not rows:
        sys.exit("no responses")

    print(f"{len(rows)} students")
    for item in CLOSED_ITEMS:
        tabulate(rows, item)
    summarize_scores(rows)
@
